{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "section_title", "parent_tag": "01RN", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\section{", "suffix": "}\n\\label{section-birational}"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:7ebf412ca4b24c568cd757d3bf205ff9b49d9fc0034a24b583abcf88b8105582", "source_text": "Birational morphisms", "source_text_hash": "sha256:0a0573c42999ec06d037c52358def222184f94142bbefc36a19fedc21f91bf95", "unit_id": "tag:01RN:title"}
{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "01RN", "placeholders": {"REF_0001": "\\ref{example-birational-not-iso-over-open}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:b2b4c543694e9f26af4153eedc9cb5608c2cf8bd4ce6eef412b136ded8169d5b", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nYou may be used to the notion of a birational map of varieties\nhaving the property that it is an isomorphism over an open subset\nof the target. However, in general a birational morphism may\nnot be an isomorphism over any nonempty open, see\nExample <REF_0001>.\nHere is the formal definition.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:aabadc490c01f9bcd6766bf7c8835225ed8d23ff6970a453a3ce6252e5320c06", "unit_id": "tag:01RN:prose-0001"}
{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "definition", "parent_tag": "01RN", "placeholders": {"LOCKED_0001": "\\begin{reference}\n\\cite[(2.2.9)]{EGA1}\n\\end{reference}", "MATH_0001": "$X$", "MATH_0002": "$Y$", "MATH_0003": "$X$", "MATH_0004": "$Y$", "MATH_0005": "$f : X \\to Y$", "MATH_0006": "$f$", "MATH_0007": "$X$", "MATH_0008": "$Y$", "MATH_0009": "$\\eta \\in X$", "MATH_0010": "$X$", "MATH_0011": "$\\mathcal{O}_{Y, f(\\eta)} \\to \\mathcal{O}_{X, \\eta}$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{enumerate}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\item", "STRUCT_0003": "\\item", "STRUCT_0004": "\\end{enumerate}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-birational}\n", "suffix": "\\end{definition}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:0ec97d6d463bbb21fb6ee988f3b8f8f471f05d22eb67fd1cb2e75e2cb013111c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:92443d4224ef04cf57511c06dfd7ca1bfdc1fb72844f643cec3f7ea62fd4c6c0", "source_text": "<LOCKED_0001>\nLet <MATH_0001>, <MATH_0002> be schemes. Assume <MATH_0003> and <MATH_0004> have finitely many\nirreducible components. We say a morphism <MATH_0005> is\n<TEXTITOPEN_0001>birational<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> if\n<STRUCT_0001>\n<STRUCT_0002> <MATH_0006> induces a bijection between the set of generic points\nof irreducible components of <MATH_0007> and the set of generic points\nof the irreducible components of <MATH_0008>, and\n<STRUCT_0003> for every generic point <MATH_0009> of an irreducible component\nof <MATH_0010> the local ring map\n<MATH_0011>\nis an isomorphism.\n<STRUCT_0004>\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:d5a4ba419d211f6bfb49a8547fde57f2397b07def0f638ece3408d39cd0be5f4", "unit_id": "tag:01RO:statement"}
{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "01RN", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$X$", "MATH_0002": "$Y$", "MATH_0003": "$X$", "MATH_0004": "$Y$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:cccb8cc7911029af1b8ceddc433a3411d1174bdcf5094f0f7c6f252bf2fd9b78", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:7b724ffdd3cb603497fe4d76103123b132d5104049e64d986240e33d73bb5f3f", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nWe will see below that the fibres of a birational morphism\nover generic points are singletons. Moreover, we will see that\nin most cases one encounters in practice the existence of a birational morphism\nbetween irreducible schemes <MATH_0001> and <MATH_0002> implies\n<MATH_0003> and <MATH_0004> are birational schemes.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:758d74c5343836e3a4fcc6b10dcd26f6a31d3f5a82662b730a26f7c9cc3d1d83", "unit_id": "tag:01RN:prose-0002"}
